I’m not in this trade, but it definitely makes me think. Volatility is extremely low, so after a strong move down, especially something like Nasdaq down 7 of 8 days and Nvidia down 7 straight, you can start picking up cheap calls as lottery tickets. You don’t need to be right often. One big winner can pay for the rest. Food for thought for myself.

@StockSusan1 Absolutely. This should never be a big position, and it’s not a daily strategy just because volatility is low. The trade only makes sense when low volatility meets extremely negative positioning and sentiment, like what we’re seeing now. That’s what creates the asymmetric setup.

Since it’s a one-day trade, you probably want to open it at the very end of the prior trading day, ideally after a very bearish session or several down days, so you capture the overnight move.

I think historical volatility is less relevant now because the Nasdaq’s composition has changed so much, especially with the much larger semiconductor weighting. I’d rather look at the volatility regime of the current index, say the last 12 months. On that basis, volatility looks pretty low.

